1
00:00:04,500 --> 00:00:09,298
Both come from one script, written in an
afternoon, that calls gh api in a loop.

2
00:00:09,338 --> 00:00:14,378
gh is GitHub's command-line program, and gh
api sends a request to GitHub's API, the

3
00:00:14,418 --> 00:00:19,035
interface that programs use to talk to
GitHub. Nobody intended either result.

4
00:00:19,075 --> 00:00:23,812
The first is a default of the API. The
second is a default of the CLI: one flag

5
00:00:23,852 --> 00:00:28,529
changed the HTTP method, the verb of the
request, without the author noticing.

6
00:00:28,869 --> 00:00:33,928
If you're going to automate anything on
GitHub, and on an AI and ML team you will,

7
00:00:33,968 --> 00:00:38,965
you need to know what the CLI does on your
behalf. This video gives you that, and

8
00:00:39,005 --> 00:00:43,007
the two defaults behind those two
lines. Keep both lines in mind.

9
00:00:43,047 --> 00:00:44,624
Each gets its explanation.

10
00:00:46,464 --> 00:00:50,186
Welcome back to Git and GitHub
Deep Mastery. Pull up a chair.

11
00:00:50,226 --> 00:00:55,121
Through Part 5 you've used gh in almost
every lab without a formal introduction.

12
00:00:55,161 --> 00:00:59,994
This video is that introduction, and it
closes the GitHub part before the gate.

13
00:01:00,334 --> 00:01:05,813
Four topics. First, what gh is: a client of
GitHub's API that knows which repository

14
00:01:05,853 --> 00:01:11,266
you're in. Second, gh pr: a full pull request
cycle from the terminal, and which of

15
00:01:11,306 --> 00:01:16,653
its commands change state. A pull request
is GitHub's proposal to merge one branch

16
00:01:16,693 --> 00:01:21,712
into another. Third, gh api: the REST
API, its method, fields, pagination and

17
00:01:21,752 --> 00:01:25,851
version header, then GraphQL at
concept level, and rate limits.

18
00:01:25,891 --> 00:01:31,107
Fourth, the integration model: webhooks
and GitHub Apps, and when an integration

19
00:01:31,147 --> 00:01:34,064
should be an App and not
your personal token.

20
00:01:34,404 --> 00:01:38,450
A word on evidence. The authors
captured no GitHub output.

21
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:44,015
The local replays show two things only:
the --help output of the installed CLI,

22
00:01:44,055 --> 00:01:48,876
version 2.88.1, and the jq program
run on a stored practice document.

23
00:01:48,916 --> 00:01:54,512
Everything else is described from the
documentation and performed by you on your

24
00:01:54,552 --> 00:01:55,921
practice repository.

25
00:01:57,761 --> 00:01:59,339
Here's what you'll walk away with.

26
00:01:59,679 --> 00:02:01,091
After this video you can:

27
00:02:01,431 --> 00:02:04,925
drive a complete feature cycle
from the terminal with gh;

28
00:02:05,265 --> 00:02:08,785
say which gh commands change
state and which only read;

29
00:02:09,125 --> 00:02:14,557
query pull request and ruleset data with
gh api and shape the result with a jq

30
00:02:14,597 --> 00:02:15,048
filter;

31
00:02:15,388 --> 00:02:21,020
explain REST API versioning, pagination
and rate limits as the section gives them;

32
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:23,575
say when an integration
should be a GitHub App.

33
00:02:25,415 --> 00:02:30,790
In one sentence. gh is a client for GitHub's
API that knows which repository you're

34
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:36,075
in, so the GitHub objects become reachable
from the directory where your Git data

35
00:02:36,115 --> 00:02:36,271
is.

36
00:02:36,611 --> 00:02:41,925
Precisely. gh isn't Git and doesn't replace
it. It calls the REST and GraphQL APIs

37
00:02:41,965 --> 00:02:47,084
with a stored token, a string that stands
for your account with a subset of its

38
00:02:47,124 --> 00:02:52,439
rights. It runs git for you where a task
needs both, as in gh repo clone and gh pr

39
00:02:52,479 --> 00:02:55,835
checkout. And it can act as
Git's credential helper.

40
00:02:56,175 --> 00:02:58,638
Four behaviors explain most surprises.

41
00:02:58,978 --> 00:03:04,406
Which repository? Inside a clone, gh
picks the repository from your remotes.

42
00:03:04,446 --> 00:03:10,307
With a fork there are two candidates, and
gh repo set-default records which one to

43
00:03:10,347 --> 00:03:16,279
use. Outside a clone, or to override, you
pass -R with owner and repository, or set

44
00:03:16,319 --> 00:03:16,855
GH_REPO.

45
00:03:17,195 --> 00:03:23,310
Machine-readable output. Most listing
commands take --json with a list of fields,

46
00:03:23,350 --> 00:03:29,465
then --jq with an expression, or --template.
JSON is a text format for structured

47
00:03:29,505 --> 00:03:34,177
data. --json without a field list
prints the available fields.

48
00:03:34,517 --> 00:03:39,155
Exit codes. 0 for success, 1 for failure,
2 when cancelled, 4 when authentication is

49
00:03:39,195 --> 00:03:43,833
required. gh pr checks adds 8 for pending
checks. Scripts should test exit codes and

50
00:03:43,873 --> 00:03:45,615
not parse human-readable output.

51
00:03:45,955 --> 00:03:51,264
Its own token. GH_TOKEN in the environment
takes precedence over the stored login.

52
00:03:51,304 --> 00:03:56,221
A stale one in a shell profile explains
"gh works in one terminal and not in

53
00:03:56,261 --> 00:03:56,808
another".

54
00:03:57,148 --> 00:04:02,617
gh pr. In one sentence: gh pr drives the
pull request object from the terminal.

55
00:04:02,657 --> 00:04:05,266
The Git work around it stays with git.

56
00:04:05,606 --> 00:04:09,784
Every flag was checked against
the help of version 2.88.1.

57
00:04:09,824 --> 00:04:12,912
None was run against
GitHub by the authors.

58
00:04:13,252 --> 00:04:17,700
Now the labels, because this
is the objective people skip.

59
00:04:17,740 --> 00:04:22,884
gh pr view, gh pr checks, gh pr
diff and gh pr status read: 🟢 SAFE.

60
00:04:22,924 --> 00:04:28,068
gh pr checkout is 🟢 SAFE too. It
changes local files and refs only.

61
00:04:28,108 --> 00:04:34,335
gh pr create is 🟡 CAUTION: it may push the
branch, and it creates a GitHub object

62
00:04:34,375 --> 00:04:35,960
that notifies people.

63
00:04:36,300 --> 00:04:42,067
The preview is --dry-run, and the textbook
notes that even the dry run may still

64
00:04:42,107 --> 00:04:47,874
push. gh pr merge, gh pr update-branch
and gh pr edit --base are 🟡 CAUTION: they

65
00:04:47,914 --> 00:04:53,608
write to the base branch, move the head
branch or change the base, and they can

66
00:04:53,648 --> 00:04:54,915
dismiss approvals.

67
00:04:55,255 --> 00:05:00,649
One flag is 🔴 DANGEROUS: gh pr merge
--admin. What it changes: the base branch,

68
00:05:00,689 --> 00:05:05,396
without the required reviews or
checks. What it can destroy: no data.

69
00:05:05,436 --> 00:05:09,868
What's lost is the review and the
checks that the rules required.

70
00:05:09,908 --> 00:05:15,371
Preview: gh pr checks --required. Recovery:
gh pr revert, and record the reason.

71
00:05:15,411 --> 00:05:20,186
Appropriate: a documented emergency,
by someone the bypass list names.

72
00:05:20,526 --> 00:05:26,014
Two details from the help text. gh pr create
takes the base from --base, else from a

73
00:05:26,054 --> 00:05:30,751
Git configuration value for the current
branch, else the default branch.

74
00:05:30,791 --> 00:05:36,213
And --match-head-commit merges only if the
head is the given commit: you merge what

75
00:05:36,253 --> 00:05:39,569
you reviewed, and not what
was pushed a second ago.

76
00:05:39,909 --> 00:05:45,816
gh api. In one sentence: everything GitHub
shows you comes from an API that you can

77
00:05:45,856 --> 00:05:51,834
call yourself. gh api adds your token, the
host and the placeholders, and prints the

78
00:05:51,874 --> 00:05:52,192
JSON.

79
00:05:52,532 --> 00:05:58,093
The REST API is a set of URLs, one per
resource. gh api takes the path and fills in

80
00:05:58,133 --> 00:06:03,356
the placeholders for owner, repository
and branch from the current repository.

81
00:06:03,696 --> 00:06:08,775
The paths and field names were checked
against the REST reference on the second of

82
00:06:08,815 --> 00:06:11,210
October 2026. The commands weren't run.

83
00:06:11,550 --> 00:06:12,829
Five rules of the road.

84
00:06:13,169 --> 00:06:17,933
First, a quick quiz. A GET request
reads, and a POST creates.

85
00:06:17,973 --> 00:06:22,895
You call gh api to list issues,
and you add one filter with -f.

86
00:06:22,935 --> 00:06:27,462
What does the CLI send? A, a
GET with a filter. B, a POST.

87
00:06:27,502 --> 00:06:28,408
Your answer?

88
00:06:33,248 --> 00:06:39,393
B, a POST. Method. GET by default, and
POST as soon as you add a field with -f or

89
00:06:39,433 --> 00:06:45,501
-F. To send parameters with a GET, you
say -X GET. That's the second line of the

90
00:06:45,541 --> 00:06:49,549
hook: a filter parameter
turned a read into a create.

91
00:06:49,889 --> 00:06:56,381
Fields. -f sends a string. -F converts true,
false, null and integers to JSON types

92
00:06:56,421 --> 00:06:57,955
and can read a file.

93
00:06:58,295 --> 00:07:03,056
Pagination. Lists return 30 items
per page unless you ask otherwise.

94
00:07:03,096 --> 00:07:07,574
--paginate follows the pages.
--slurp wraps them into one array.

95
00:07:07,614 --> 00:07:09,975
That's the first line of the hook.

96
00:07:10,315 --> 00:07:16,982
Versioning. The REST API is versioned by
date through the X-GitHub-Api-Version

97
00:07:17,022 --> 00:07:23,947
header. Without the header, a request gets
version 2022-11-28, which is supported

98
00:07:23,987 --> 00:07:30,482
until the tenth of March 2028. 2026-03-10
is the first version with breaking

99
00:07:30,522 --> 00:07:37,705
changes. An unsupported version answers 410
Gone. The CLI pins its own REST calls to

100
00:07:37,745 --> 00:07:44,929
2022-11-28 since version 2.87.0. In a script
that must keep working, send the header

101
00:07:44,969 --> 00:07:45,874
explicitly.

102
00:07:46,214 --> 00:07:51,641
Not found means "not for you". For a private
resource and a token without access,

103
00:07:51,681 --> 00:07:57,243
the API answers 404, not 403, in the
documentation's words "to avoid confirming the

104
00:07:57,283 --> 00:07:59,605
existence of private repositories".

105
00:07:59,945 --> 00:08:05,034
Every gh api call with GET is 🟢 SAFE. Every
call with POST, PATCH, PUT or DELETE,

106
00:08:05,074 --> 00:08:10,163
and every GraphQL mutation, is 🔴 DANGEROUS,
and section 15.22 gives the reason in

107
00:08:10,203 --> 00:08:14,912
one line: it changes whatever the
endpoint says, with all your permissions.

108
00:08:14,952 --> 00:08:20,232
The preview is always the GET first. The
recovery depends on the endpoint, and often

109
00:08:20,272 --> 00:08:21,055
there's none.

110
00:08:21,395 --> 00:08:26,899
GraphQL is the second API: one endpoint,
a typed schema, and a query that names

111
00:08:26,939 --> 00:08:32,021
exactly the fields wanted, so one call
can replace several REST requests.

112
00:08:32,061 --> 00:08:35,951
gh api graphql -f query=
followed by the query calls it.

113
00:08:35,991 --> 00:08:39,951
A GraphQL query only reads,
although it's sent as a POST.

114
00:08:39,991 --> 00:08:43,320
Some features exist in
only one of the two APIs.

115
00:08:43,660 --> 00:08:48,998
Rate limits. With no authentication:
60 requests per hour, per IP address.

116
00:08:49,038 --> 00:08:53,795
An authenticated user, which is
your token and gh: 5,000 per hour.

117
00:08:53,835 --> 00:08:59,609
A GitHub App installation: 5,000 per hour
at minimum, 15,000 on Enterprise Cloud

118
00:08:59,649 --> 00:09:04,987
organizations. The GITHUB_TOKEN in a
workflow: 1,000 requests per hour per

119
00:09:05,027 --> 00:09:05,787
repository.

120
00:09:06,127 --> 00:09:12,419
Secondary limits apply on top: at most 100
concurrent requests, and a points budget

121
00:09:12,459 --> 00:09:18,828
per minute. Exceeding a limit returns 403
or 429. So a 403 isn't always a permission

122
00:09:18,868 --> 00:09:22,872
problem. gh api rate_limit
tells you where you stand.

123
00:09:23,212 --> 00:09:28,418
Webhooks and GitHub Apps. Polling asks GitHub
repeatedly whether something happened.

124
00:09:28,458 --> 00:09:33,602
A webhook reverses the direction: you
register a URL and the events you care about,

125
00:09:33,642 --> 00:09:37,911
and GitHub sends an HTTP request
with a JSON payload when one occurs.

126
00:09:37,951 --> 00:09:42,657
A push webhook carries the ref and the
commit IDs before and after the push.

127
00:09:42,697 --> 00:09:47,779
The textbook's remark is worth repeating:
that's the information of a reflog line,

128
00:09:47,819 --> 00:09:49,340
delivered to your server.

129
00:09:49,680 --> 00:09:53,102
A GitHub App is the identity
an integration should have.

130
00:09:53,142 --> 00:09:57,614
It's installed on chosen repositories.
It holds fine-grained permissions.

131
00:09:57,654 --> 00:10:02,683
It receives webhooks. It acts with installation
tokens that expire after one hour.

132
00:10:02,723 --> 00:10:07,505
And it isn't tied to a person, so it
doesn't stop working when someone leaves.

133
00:10:07,845 --> 00:10:12,937
The alternatives are a personal token, which
acts as you everywhere you have access,

134
00:10:12,977 --> 00:10:17,764
and a machine user. One fact for tool
writers: the API for check runs and check

135
00:10:17,804 --> 00:10:22,713
suites is available only to GitHub Apps.
The older commit statuses can be created

136
00:10:22,753 --> 00:10:23,813
with Write access.

137
00:10:24,153 --> 00:10:28,781
One thing here is unverified. Webhook
delivery retries and signature validation

138
00:10:28,821 --> 00:10:33,391
weren't researched for the Phase 0 report,
and aren't described in the course.

139
00:10:35,231 --> 00:10:38,130
There's one API. Everything
else is a client.

140
00:10:38,470 --> 00:10:43,289
The browser is a client. gh is a
client. A script with curl is a client.

141
00:10:43,329 --> 00:10:48,958
A GitHub App is a client. What differs
between them is the credential each presents,

142
00:10:48,998 --> 00:10:51,995
and therefore what each
may do and how often.

143
00:10:52,335 --> 00:10:55,849
So for any automation question,
ask three things in this order.

144
00:10:55,889 --> 00:10:58,726
Which endpoint am I calling,
and with which method?

145
00:10:58,766 --> 00:11:02,901
Which credential is making the call,
and what else can that credential do?

146
00:11:02,941 --> 00:11:07,358
And what happens to this automation when
the person who wrote it changes teams?

147
00:11:07,698 --> 00:11:12,414
The model breaks slightly for gh,
because gh is two clients in one.

148
00:11:12,454 --> 00:11:16,532
It talks to the API, and it
also runs git on your machine.

149
00:11:16,872 --> 00:11:22,620
Try it now, thirty seconds, on paper. Two
commands: gh pr checkout and gh pr create.

150
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:28,408
For each, write what it does to Git data on
your machine and what it does on GitHub.

151
00:11:28,448 --> 00:11:29,097
I'll wait.

152
00:11:33,937 --> 00:11:38,995
gh pr checkout creates a local branch:
it changes local files and refs only.

153
00:11:39,035 --> 00:11:44,564
gh pr create may push, which is Git data,
and it creates a pull request, which is a

154
00:11:44,604 --> 00:11:50,132
GitHub object. When you read a gh command,
split it in your head into the part that

155
00:11:50,172 --> 00:11:54,023
touches Git data and the part
that touches GitHub objects.

156
00:11:55,863 --> 00:11:59,387
Three clients, one API, and
a credential on every arrow.

157
00:11:59,427 --> 00:12:03,841
The last arrow points the other way.
Only the App is called by GitHub.

158
00:12:03,881 --> 00:12:05,559
The other two can only ask.

159
00:12:07,399 --> 00:12:09,119
Step 1: the families.

160
00:12:15,459 --> 00:12:19,151
Find four lines. pr, where
you'll spend most of your time.

161
00:12:19,191 --> 00:12:24,170
api, the one command that reaches everything
else. ruleset, described as "View

162
00:12:24,210 --> 00:12:29,512
info", which you met in video 134. And under
the Actions heading, run and workflow,

163
00:12:29,552 --> 00:12:32,343
which belong to the next
part of the course.

164
00:12:32,683 --> 00:12:35,621
Step 2: the release
command explains itself.

165
00:12:38,961 --> 00:12:43,877
Read the first sentence of the help text
aloud: if a matching Git tag doesn't yet

166
00:12:43,917 --> 00:12:48,649
exist, one will automatically get created
from the latest state of the default

167
00:12:48,689 --> 00:12:53,727
branch. That's why section 15.22 labels gh
release create without --verify-tag as 🔴

168
00:12:53,767 --> 00:12:57,031
DANGEROUS, and with --verify-tag
--draft as 🟡 CAUTION.

169
00:12:57,071 --> 00:13:01,926
The textbook's production case: a release
script on the day the version variable

170
00:13:01,966 --> 00:13:06,760
holds a typo. --verify-tag turns that
into an error before anything is created.

171
00:13:07,100 --> 00:13:09,772
Step 3: the flags of gh api.

172
00:13:10,112 --> 00:13:14,934
Before the output: which flag's default
is "GET", and which two flags will change

173
00:13:14,974 --> 00:13:18,055
that default without mentioning
it? Say it out loud.

174
00:13:22,895 --> 00:13:28,244
--method says default "GET". --field
and --raw-field add parameters.

175
00:13:28,284 --> 00:13:34,664
And --paginate says what it does in plain
words: make additional HTTP requests to

176
00:13:34,704 --> 00:13:36,804
fetch all pages of results.

177
00:13:37,144 --> 00:13:42,132
Three subcommands: check, list,
view. No create, no edit.

178
00:13:42,472 --> 00:13:47,672
Step 4: rehearse the filters offline.
Replay labs/run ch15/jq-rehearsal.

179
00:13:47,712 --> 00:13:53,785
The input is a practice document written
for the course, with the field names of the

180
00:13:53,825 --> 00:13:59,826
pull request list. It isn't output from
GitHub. And jq is a command-line filter for

181
00:13:59,866 --> 00:14:00,190
JSON.

182
00:14:00,530 --> 00:14:05,995
.[] iterates over the array. -r
prints strings without quotes.

183
00:14:06,035 --> 00:14:11,767
With gh api, the same filter goes
after --jq and no -r is needed.

184
00:14:12,107 --> 00:14:15,906
The backslash-parenthesis form
interpolates a field into a string.

185
00:14:15,946 --> 00:14:18,930
Three pull requests, two
authors, two base branches.

186
00:14:19,270 --> 00:14:23,564
Three pull requests. One is a draft,
one targets a release branch.

187
00:14:23,604 --> 00:14:26,584
Which number is printed?
Make your prediction.

188
00:14:31,424 --> 00:14:34,698
select keeps the elements for
which the condition holds.

189
00:14:34,738 --> 00:14:37,124
Only number 14 is ready and targets main.

190
00:14:37,464 --> 00:14:40,697
And building a new object per
element, with a renamed field.

191
00:14:41,037 --> 00:14:45,550
Step 5: the Git half of a
feature cycle. Replay labs/run

192
00:14:45,590 --> 00:14:52,217
ch15/lab-25-1-feature-cycle. It runs against
a local bare server, so it shows only

193
00:14:52,257 --> 00:14:56,527
what Git does. The platform
steps appear as comments.

194
00:14:56,867 --> 00:15:00,787
git push -u is 🟡 CAUTION: it
creates a ref on the server.

195
00:15:00,827 --> 00:15:04,262
On GitHub, the next command
would be gh pr create.

196
00:15:04,602 --> 00:15:09,094
A review comment asks for documentation.
You commit and push again.

197
00:15:09,134 --> 00:15:13,017
The pull request now has two
commits: c0b29a8 and 8e723d1.

198
00:15:13,357 --> 00:15:16,610
The pull request was
squash-merged on the platform.

199
00:15:16,650 --> 00:15:21,387
git pull --ff-only is 🟡 CAUTION: it
fetches and moves your branch forward.

200
00:15:21,427 --> 00:15:26,746
On main there's one new commit, 9477a3f,
and its subject ends with the pull request

201
00:15:26,786 --> 00:15:28,167
number in parentheses.

202
00:15:28,507 --> 00:15:33,322
Here's that moment as a graph. Before the
pull, your branch feature/list-names is

203
00:15:33,362 --> 00:15:34,880
two commits ahead of main.

204
00:15:35,220 --> 00:15:40,730
After the pull, main moves to the squash
commit. Your two commits, 8e723d1 and

205
00:15:40,770 --> 00:15:42,936
c0b29a8, aren't in its history.

206
00:15:43,276 --> 00:15:48,702
git fetch --prune is 🟡 CAUTION: it deletes
stale remote-tracking refs and their

207
00:15:48,742 --> 00:15:54,376
reflogs. The branch was merged on the
platform. Will git branch -d agree that it's

208
00:15:54,416 --> 00:15:55,898
merged? Say yes or no.

209
00:15:58,238 --> 00:16:03,277
It refuses: "not fully merged". After
a squash merge your two commits aren't

210
00:16:03,317 --> 00:16:06,752
ancestors of main. Git checks
ancestry, not content.

211
00:16:06,792 --> 00:16:12,166
So the script checks content: git diff
--quiet between main and the branch prints

212
00:16:12,206 --> 00:16:13,168
"same content".

213
00:16:13,508 --> 00:16:18,606
Only then comes git branch -D, which is
🔴 DANGEROUS, so the five answers first.

214
00:16:18,646 --> 00:16:23,809
What it changes: it deletes a ref and its
reflog. What it can destroy: your only

215
00:16:23,849 --> 00:16:29,077
name for commits that are on no other
branch. How to preview: the tree comparison

216
00:16:29,117 --> 00:16:34,410
you saw. How to recover: git branch with
the name and the ID, and the ID is in the

217
00:16:34,450 --> 00:16:39,874
output, c0b29a8. When it's appropriate:
here, after you've shown that the content is

218
00:16:39,914 --> 00:16:40,394
on main.

219
00:16:40,734 --> 00:16:45,295
And the picture after the deletion: the
branch name is gone, and no branch reaches

220
00:16:45,335 --> 00:16:46,697
the two commits any more.

221
