225
00:17:10,382 --> 00:17:15,441
because the lab shell switches off the
system configuration where the credential

226
00:17:15,481 --> 00:17:20,667
helper lives. The interface changes. The
lab text and the linked documentation are

227
00:17:20,707 --> 00:17:21,560
the reference.

228
00:17:21,900 --> 00:17:27,277
The cycle is: an issue, a branch, commits,
a pull request whose body closes the

229
00:17:27,317 --> 00:17:33,037
issue, a review comment, a squash merge,
local clean-up, then an annotated tag and a

230
00:17:33,077 --> 00:17:38,797
release with --verify-tag. After each gh
command, say aloud which object was created

231
00:17:38,837 --> 00:17:43,322
and in which layer. gh issue create:
a GitHub object, no Git data.

232
00:17:43,662 --> 00:17:49,503
git push: Git data on the server. gh pr
create: a GitHub object, and according to

233
00:17:49,543 --> 00:17:55,310
Chapter 17 also refs under refs/pull/. gh
pr merge --squash: a new commit on the

234
00:17:55,350 --> 00:18:01,335
server's main, made by GitHub. Keep the
browser closed until the end, then open the

235
00:18:01,375 --> 00:18:06,634
pull request page once and compare it
with what the JSON fields told you.

236
00:18:08,474 --> 00:18:10,123
Five mistakes to watch for.

237
00:18:10,463 --> 00:18:16,408
A script reports exactly 30 items. Root
cause: lists return 30 items per page and

238
00:18:16,448 --> 00:18:18,994
the script did not pass --paginate.

239
00:18:19,334 --> 00:18:25,016
A script created something by accident. Root
cause: a field flag switched the method

240
00:18:25,056 --> 00:18:27,809
from GET to POST; -X GET keeps it a read.

241
00:18:28,149 --> 00:18:33,095
gh acts on the wrong one of fork and
upstream. Root cause: with two remotes there

242
00:18:33,135 --> 00:18:37,588
are two candidates, and no default was
recorded with gh repo set-default.

243
00:18:37,928 --> 00:18:44,069
Reading a 404 as "the repository does not
exist". Root cause: for a private resource

244
00:18:44,109 --> 00:18:48,337
and a token without access the
API answers 404 on purpose.

245
00:18:48,677 --> 00:18:52,705
Building tooling on a personal
token and on human-readable output.

246
00:18:52,745 --> 00:18:57,513
Root cause: the token acts as one person
everywhere and stops when that person

247
00:18:57,553 --> 00:19:02,320
leaves, and the text output is not a
stable interface; use --json, the version

248
00:19:02,360 --> 00:19:03,861
header, and a GitHub App.

249
00:19:05,701 --> 00:19:11,000
Now, out of the lab. The textbook's case.
A nightly job lists the pull requests of

250
00:19:11,040 --> 00:19:15,557
sixty repositories with unauthenticated
curl. It worked from a laptop.

251
00:19:15,597 --> 00:19:20,635
It fails on the shared CI runner, where
the sixty requests per hour of that IP

252
00:19:20,675 --> 00:19:26,039
address are spent before the job starts.
Unauthenticated limits were lowered on the

253
00:19:26,079 --> 00:19:29,425
eighth of May 2025, anonymous
HTTPS clones included.

254
00:19:29,765 --> 00:19:34,857
The tempting repair is a retry loop. The
textbook's verdict: the fix is an identity

255
00:19:34,897 --> 00:19:40,050
for the job, not a retry loop. For an
evaluation dashboard on an AI and ML team that

256
00:19:40,090 --> 00:19:45,244
reads pull request data every night, the
identity is a GitHub App installed on those

257
00:19:45,284 --> 00:19:48,830
sixty repositories with read
permission for pull requests.

258
00:19:48,870 --> 00:19:53,962
The job then has its own rate limit, and
it survives the departure of whoever wrote

259
00:19:54,002 --> 00:19:54,147
it.

